  "lineage": "Afro-Puerto Rican drum-and-dance ritual tradition traced directly to West African Yoruba and Ashanti rhythmic-drum-and-call-response heritage brought through 17-18th-century enslaved African labor, with Loíza (the Black-majority town on the northeast coast), Mayagüez, Ponce, and Cataño as the bomba towns; played on the bomba barril (single-headed barrel drum carved from rum cask) with the soloist drummer (subidor or primo) tracing the dancers steps in real-time dialogue while a secondary drum (buleador) maintains rhythmic foundation, plus maracas and cuá (struck wood block) — preserved through the Cepeda and Ayala families and modern groups like Los Pleneros de la 21",
